WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.491
Film Courage: We’ve heard screenwriters
use the phrase ‘Cracking The Story,’ so

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:05.649
what does this mean exactly?

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:11.030
Gary Goldstein: Cracking The Story is (just
to continue from what we were talking about)

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:16.170
you have the beginnings of a story but then
you beat out the story, you create the whole

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.580
story, you role out the carpet, basically
the whole story, so you don’t really crack

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:25.140
the story and it may be more of a TV term
than a film term (I’m not sure) but you

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:31.730
don’t crack the story until you are able
to tell the story from start to finish and

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.420
any of the holes in it are filled and that’s
my interpretation of it.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.660
The concept of cracking something is like
“Oh!

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.010
That was what was missing.”

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.280
We got it and now we cracked the story and
the rest of it just falls into place.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.610
So there’s that, but also just the concept
of being able to tell the story from start

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.610
to finish.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:56.530
If I send in a pitch (let’s say) and I write
up a pitch to send into a producer or network

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:03.120
or something for a movie or TV show pilot
or something, I may send a premise line and

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.020
see if the general premise line is of interest.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:06.020
And they may say “Oh, no.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.050
We are already doing something like that.”

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.780
Or “That’s great, give me more.”

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.470
I will give them the story from start to finish.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.900
It doesn’t have to be very long.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.020
It can be a page and a half that I’m writing
but at least I’m not just giving them preamble.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.450
I’m not just giving them the first act of
something.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.760
I’m not just giving them like a tease.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.060
I’m basically telling them, cracking that
story for them, telling them where that story

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.210
is going to go from start to finish.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.120
Not necessarily in great detail.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:36.940
Sometimes in more detail but it’s really
about being able to show that this is an entire

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:37.940
story.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.770
This is where this will move and then we can
fill it in and amplify it as we go along.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.540
So it’s really being able to tell a story
from start to finish, for me anyway.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.360
That’s my interpretation of it.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.340
Film Courage: What about those writers who
say sometimes they begin something without

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.340
knowing the ending?

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.340
Gary Goldstein: Well, I’ve done that.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.381
Film Courage: Oh, you have?

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:59.230
Gary Goldstein: I’ve done that or where
the ending has changed a lot.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.990
Again with April, May and June (this play),
I didn’t know.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.690
I didn’t know what they were going to find.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.130
I didn’t know that at the end of the first
act they were going to find…that the sisters

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.520
were going to find something, that it just
became obvious, it became the thing that was

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.520
driving me.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.550
I didn’t even realize it.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.530
And I didn’t know what that thing was going
to be until I thought about it.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.020
So that was a case where I really didn’t
know.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.180
I’ve definitely written things where if
you’re writing a romantic comedy, generally

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.380
the couple will get back together in the end.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.040
There’s all of that, kind of some givens
but sometimes you don’t know.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.170
Frankly, I do think it’s better to know
or at least have some idea.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.471
It’s like getting in a car and saying “Well,
I know how to get halfway there but I don’t

00:02:38.471 --> 00:02:40.140
really know where I’m going after that?”

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:46.190
You’re going to be stuck in the middle of
nowhere so it’s not dissimilar with a story.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:53.300
I prefer to know where I was going and I think
certainly before you start sitting down and

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.130
writing it, you should have a pretty good
idea of where you’re going and if it does

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.550
evolve or it makes changes along the way,
great.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:00.670
You know, work with it.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.490
See where it takes you.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.030
These stories unfold on your own sometimes.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:11.630
But I think it’s helpful, like I said, I’m
a big believer in structure and having the

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.130
beats out before I start writing something
from start to finish but we can be surprised

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:19.500
and I bet there’s definitely been times
when I’ve not known exactly where I was

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.110
going until I got there.

